00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:22.230 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i think the mit museum is a unique place  at least in my experience mit itself of  course is a very special research  institute not like a lot of other  universities and the museum is the only  real institution that mit has to  represent the whole of what it does to  the rest of the world  all museums are  cultural centers we have the culture of  mit  what makes it special what makes it  interesting there are no written records  for most of the things that we have  just the thing itself  so we're part archaeologist part  anthropologist part historian and we  hope to imbue those same qualities in  our visitors every museum of science and  technology in some sense should be a  museum of ideas perhaps what makes this  one special is that many of the ideas we  deal with are ideas in the making ideas  that we're not sure about and some of  the uncertainties some of the challenges  of the research process are also present  here and so they should be  [Music]  the mit museum is  home to an extraordinary set of objects  that define the 19th 20th and 21st  century  doc edgerton's negatives and artifacts  are among the most precious collections  they document a technology high-speed  stroboscopic photography but they also  document the desire to stop time  to see what is unseen  [Music]  the mit museum also has the world's  finest collection of holograms  many of them are on display here in the  museum but much of the collection is  also accessible on our website  [Music]  another collection that we have is  probably the world's best collection of  robotics that were created by  university-based research robots like  cog or kismet the humanoid social robots  very good kismet  there is no other place where you can  see two three dozen objects that tell  the history of artificial intelligence  as well as our collection does  [Music]  the heart nautical collections is a  unique archive of 350 000 items the  interesting thing that is  perhaps unique in all nautical  technology collections  is that we have this amazing amount of  historic data that goes back to the mid  19th century and our collections  continue right up to the present we are  actively collecting technology being  developed today for example the side  scan sonar  systems that we have behind me this is a  type of technology that is equivalent to  the development of the x-ray in medical  imaging this allows us to see details on  the ocean floor that were never before  capable  one of the things that's most vital  about engaging people with interest in  ocean science  is the giant frontier that it represents  i mean we know more about the dark side  of the moon than we know about the ocean  the mit museum is also home to one of  the important architectural study  collections in the country this  documents the first hundred years of  architectural instruction at mit which  was the first academic program in  architecture in the country the recent  exhibition of front and back in our  compton gallery  featured our earliest architectural  drawing attributed to a renaissance  architecture involved sorry perutzi  dating from the 16th century we pulled  it apart looked at all the layers of the  drawing subjected it to scientific  analysis we also took the drawing to  the design labs to 3d model the drawing  to look at  contemporary approaches to  a renaissance drawing in other words we  had really basically no assumptions when  we went into the project  and we came out with a lot more  questions than we started with  but for us that was the process was what  was interesting  soapbox is a new program for adults in  the mit museum the aim is to engage the  wider community a little more closely  in the debate that needs to go on in the  21st century about how we're going to  live and live better with the fruits of  science and technology the issue of  global warming has risen to the top of  the public's agenda  of environmental concerns we've done  everything from biomedical issues  through to uh ways in which new  nanotechnologies new materials will be  changing everyday life after the  speakers have presented the issues for  the evening the audience breaks up into  small discussion groups and brainstorms  what they've heard and it's on the basis  of what the discussion groups come back  with that we organize the rest of the  conversation  so what we're trying to say is here's a  place where you can take part in the  conversations that in the end will help  shape the way we and our children live  in the future  we'd like people to come to think of the  mit museum as an innovative place itself  one of the innovations that we are  leading at the moment is to create a new  cambridge science festival  the idea is to have nine or ten days  each year in the spring  when we open up the entire world of  science and technology in this city  [Music]  what we're planning is a whole series of  events scattered across the community  we're talking about plays science  writing events  exhibits a careers day for young people  and the hope is  that anybody who's awake in cambridge  for that 10 days will realize that the  science festival is on the question is  not so much  when will we build a robot  that is a human being we have this  extraordinary situation where we can  work with colleagues with grad students  and others to try to open up what  they're doing for the wider community  and it's that notion that really is at  the heart of what we're trying to do  with the mit museum as we look forward  that means that we need a lot more space  and it means we also want to expand the  range of what we offer into subject  areas where the museum historically  hasn't done very much we are in the  middle of an exciting renovation of a  ground floor gallery space  that will open up our  collections and our programs and our  exhibitions quite literally to an  outside audience on mass avenue for the  first time we now have ground level  visibility for the museum it's an  important step for us and that space  will allow us to have new exhibitions  and new dedicated public program spaces  which will mean that we can do  things like soapbox much more easily  when uncoiled the dna in one cell of  your body is six feet long science is  something for everyone technology and  science matter that's the most important  message that i hope people leave this  museum with we do see ourselves as  having that kind of inclusivity and that  accessibility agenda an agenda to try to  draw people in to benefit from to enjoy  and if they want to challenge what goes  on in a place like mit  [Music]  you

